        "content": "<p>ilyas-mallah opened <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14778\">PR #14778</a> from <code>ilyas-mallah:x64-fcvt-to-small-int</code> to <code>bytecodealliance:main</code>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compiling fcvt_to_{u,s}int{,_sat} with an i8 or i16 result panics on x64 in <a href=\"http://emit.rs\">emit.rs</a>. The lowering rules accept those types, but the conversion sequences only emit 32-bit and 64-bit forms. aarch64, riscv64 and s390x already lower them. Wasm never produces these results, so only CLIF reaches the panic.</p>\n<p>This converts to i32, then clamps the result or traps with int_ovf when it's out of range. The unsigned forms can use the signed conversion, since anything at 2^31 or above already saturates or traps there.</p>\n<p>x86_64 now runs the existing i8 and i16 runtests. Runtests can't expect a trap, so I also ran 122 NaN and out-of-range inputs natively, and every one trapped. The run line I fixed in fcvt-sat-small.clif named the signed function under the unsigned one. <code>test run</code> ignores the name and calls the function above it, so it never failed.</p>\n<p>Fixes #4897.<br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>",
